{
  format: 1
  name: seed-and-bundle-supply-same-address-rejected
  suite: static
  spec: ["#package-structure", "§4.1", "#modules", "§13.13"]
  note: '''
    §4.1: "`$data` is an alias of `$seed`; a definition declaring both `$data` and
    `$seed` is rejected, and an address supplied by both `$seed` (or `$data`) and
    `$bundle` is a static load error."

    The two members carry DISTINCT OWNERSHIP, which is what makes the overlap
    undecidable rather than merely redundant. §4.1: `$seed` "is starting data the
    application's users own once instantiated", applied on a later update "only where
    the seeded address is then absent", while `$bundle` "is package-authoritative
    content the package itself maintains across releases", three-way merged on every
    update. §13.13 gives the same address two contradictory update rules — never
    overwrite it, and reconcile it against the new bundle — so a package that supplies
    one address from both has no admissible update semantics, and §4.1 refuses the
    DEFINITION rather than picking a side at update time.

    Here the collection row `/notes[n1]` is supplied by both. The row's own body
    differs between the two, but that is incidental: the rule is about the address,
    not about the values disagreeing.
  '''

  package: {
    $liasse: 1
    $app: "t.dualowner@1.0.0"
    $model: {
      notes: {
        $key: "id"
        id: "text"
        body: "text"
      }
    }
    $seed: { notes: { n1: { body: "owned by the instance's users" } } }
    $bundle: { notes: { n1: { body: "owned by the package" } } }
  }

  expect: {
    outcome: invalid
    violates: ["#package-structure", "§4.1"]
    detail: "the row `notes.n1` is supplied by both `$seed` and `$bundle`; an address is owned by exactly one of them"
  }
}
